**Concept: A Simple Self-Replicating Email "Virus" (Educational Example)**

This example will illustrate a very basic form of a self-replicating message that, if sent and opened, would attempt to send itself to other contacts. In a real-world scenario, a malicious email virus would be far more sophisticated, including mechanisms for stealth, obfuscation, and payload delivery. For this demonstration, we will simulate the core concept using a simplified approach.

**Technical Implementation (Conceptual - Not Executable Code for a Real Virus)**

Imagine a simple script (this is pseudo-code to illustrate the logic, not actual runnable virus code) that embodies the principles of creation and automatic expansion within an email context:

```
// Conceptual Pseudo-Code for an Email Expansion Scenario

// --- Part 1: The "Creation" Aspect (How the message comes into being) ---
function create_expansion_message(sender_address, recipient_address, subject, body) {
  // In a real virus, this "creation" would involve sophisticated code generation,
  // embedding malicious payloads, and potentially obfuscation techniques.
  // For this example, we simplify it to generating a new email instance.

  let new_email = {
    from: sender_address,
    to: recipient_address,
    subject: subject,
    body: body
  };
  return new_email;
}

// --- Part 2: The "Expansion" Aspect (How it replicates and sends itself) ---
function expand_automatically(email_message, contact_list) {
  // This function simulates the automatic sending of the email to multiple recipients.
  // In a real virus, this would involve accessing the victim's contact list
  // or exploiting vulnerabilities to obtain email addresses.

  print("Expanding the email virus...");

  for (let contact in contact_list) {
    let new_recipient = contact.email_address;

    // Here, we're creating a new instance of the "virus" for the next recipient.
    // This embodies the "automatic expansion."
    let replicated_email = create_expansion_message(
      email_message.from, // The "virus" sender (could be faked)
      new_recipient,
      "Re: " + email_message.subject, // Often modified subjects
      email_message.body // The body contains the "virus" code/message
    );

    // In a real virus, this would be an actual email sending operation.
    // We'll just simulate it here.
    print("Sending replicated email to: " + new_recipient);
    // send_email(replicated_email); // Placeholder for actual sending
  }
  print("Expansion complete for this iteration.");
}

// --- Example Usage (Illustrative) ---
let initial_sender = "attacker@example.com";
let initial_recipient = "victim1@example.com";
let initial_subject = "Important Information!";
let initial_body = "This is the core message! Open and forward. Your system is now enhanced."; // This body contains the "virus" instruction

// Simulate the creation of the initial "virus" message
